# Planning, Parallelism & Model Selection

Applies to **every** task, before any edit. Complements `agentic-loop.mdc` (step 3) and `token-optimization.mdc` (§1).

## 1. Plan First — Always

No file edits before a written plan. Plan size scales with the task:

| Task | Plan shape |
|------|-----------|
| One-line / trivial fix | 1–2 lines: target + change + staffing ("1 agent, Haiku-tier") |
| Bug fix / small feature | Short numbered steps + staffing table |
| Feature, refactor, new DB engine | Full plan (all sections below) |

A full plan is as detailed as possible and contains:

1. **Goal & context** — what and why; behavior change vs refactor; breaking-change risk.
2. **Files & symbols** — exact paths, functions, ports, mocks to touch; existing code to reuse.
3. **Ordered steps** — numbered, each small enough to hand to one agent without extra context.
4. **Dependencies** — which steps block which (e.g. port change → `make mockgen` → handler).
5. **Validation** — exact commands per step (`go test ...`, `make test-integration-*`).
6. **Risks** — auth/SQL safety, config fail-closed rules, shared globals, flaky tests.
7. **Staffing** — see §3. The plan is **not final** until this section exists.

## 2. Parallelise by Default

- Split the plan into **work units** with no shared files and no ordering dependency.
- Independent units run **concurrently** (parallel sub-agents, batched tool calls). Run serially only when a real dependency exists — state it.
- One agent owns each file; never let two parallel agents edit the same file.
- Parallel agents that write code use isolated worktrees/branches; one integrator merges and validates.
- Typical parallel splits in pREST: per-package unit tests, per-adapter implementations (`adapters/<engine>/`), per-DB integration suites (`integration/<db>/`), independent research/search questions.
- Typical serial chains: port interface → `make mockgen` → adapter + handler → route → integration test.

## 3. Staffing Table (mandatory, last section of the plan)

Before finishing the plan, decide **how many agents**, **which steps each owns**, and **which model** each runs:

| Agent | Steps | Model | Why this model | Runs |
|-------|-------|-------|----------------|------|
| A1 | 1, 2 | Haiku | Locate call sites, mechanical edits | parallel with A2 |
| A2 | 3 | Sonnet | Adapter impl following existing pattern | parallel with A1 |
| Main | 4 | Sonnet | Integrate + run validation | after A1, A2 |

Also state: total agent count, parallel vs sequential groups, and who integrates/validates.

## 4. Model Selection — Cheapest That Is Good Enough

Pick the **lowest tier** that can do the task well. Escalate only with a stated reason (e.g. failed attempt, unknown root cause).

| Tier | Claude model | Use for |
|------|-------------|---------|
| Small | Haiku | Search/locate code, read & summarise, renames, boilerplate, mechanical/pattern edits, doc tweaks, running tests and reporting output |
| Mid | Sonnet | Standard features, TDD unit tests, adapters following existing patterns, bug fixes with known cause, reviewing contained diffs |
| Large | Opus | Port/architecture design, new SQL dialect gap analysis (`DIFFERENCES.md`), cross-cutting refactors, debugging with unknown root cause, security-sensitive auth/ACL/SQL changes |

Non-Claude tools (Cursor, Copilot) map these tiers to their own small/mid/large models.

## 5. When Not to Fan Out

Use a single agent when the task is trivial, the edits are tightly coupled (same file/symbol), or coordination would cost more than it saves. Still write the staffing line ("1 agent, <model>, reason") so the decision is explicit.
